Q: Script returns different Google Maps on different computers with same screen resolution A: <p>Google Maps does not guarantee identical visual output for a given zoom level across different computers, even when screen resolution, browser, and viewport size are the same.</p>
<p>This happens because Google Maps renders tiles based on device pixel ratio (DPI scaling), GPU/graphics rendering, and internal tile rounding, not purely on screen resolution. As a result, the same zoom level (e.g., z=11) can appear more zoomed in on some machines.</p>
<p>There is no supported way to force a fixed zoom to look identical everywhere.</p>
<p>Correct solution: Use <code>fitBounds()</code> instead of a fixed zoom so the same geographic area is always visible, regardless of device.</p>
<pre><code>var bounds = new google.maps.LatLngBounds(
  new google.maps.LatLng(48.1, -72.8),
  new google.maps.LatLng(49.0, -71.4)
);
map.fitBounds(bounds);
</code></pre>
